 the \\(n\\) diagonal holes, and satisfy the row/column condition.\n\n6. **Conclusion.** The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable; thus the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\) for \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by \\(p(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\nDefine the two subsets of covered squares  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all covered unit squares is \\(L\\cup R\\), while the uncovered squares are the \\(n\\) points \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1.  (Tiles lie completely in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\))\n\nLet a tile \\(T\\) be a rectangle with row interval \\([a,b]\\) and column interval \\([c,d]\\) (\\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the tile occupies columns \\(c,\\dots ,d\\).  Since the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(p(i)\\), \\(T\\) cannot contain that square; therefore either \\(d<p(i)\\) (the whole tile lies left of the uncovered square) or \\(c>p(i)\\) (the whole tile lies right of it).  The inequalities are mutually exclusive, and because \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) are independent of \\(i\\), the same inequality must hold for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\).  Hence either  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in[a,b]}p(i),\n\\]\n\nwhich implies \\(T\\subseteq L\\), or  \n\n\\[\nc>\\max_{i\\in[a,b]}p(i),\n\\]\n\nwhich implies \\(T\\subseteq R\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently the collection of tiles splits into two disjoint families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_L\\subseteq L,\\qquad \\mathcal T_R\\subseteq R,\n\\]\n\nwhich together cover \\(L\\cup R\\) without overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.  (At least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(L\\))\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the square  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\,p(i)-1)\n\\]\n\nis the rightmost element of \\(L\\) in that row; we call \\(\\ell_i\\) an *inner corner* of \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\); therefore there are precisely \\(n-1\\) inner corners \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  Suppose \\(Q\\) contained two distinct inner corners \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(\\ell_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  Then the column interval of \\(Q\\) contains both columns \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(k)-1\\).  Since the column interval is contiguous, it also contains every column between them.  If \\(p(i)-1>p(k)-1\\), then column \\(p(i)-1\\) belongs to \\(Q\\) in row \\(k\\), but row \\(k\\) contains no square of \\(L\\) in that column (its left part ends at column \\(p(k)-1\\)).  This contradicts \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  The case \\(p(k)-1>p(i)-1\\) is symmetric.  Hence **each rectangle of \\(L\\) can contain at most one inner corner**.\n\nSince \\(L\\) possesses \\(n-1\\) inner corners, any covering of \\(L\\) by rectangles must contain at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.  (At least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(R\\))\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the square  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,\\,p(i)+1)\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost element of \\(R\\) in that row; these \\(n-1\\) squares are the inner corners of \\(R\\).  As in Lemma\u202f2, a rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one such inner corner, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 4.  (Global lower bound)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the tiles are partitioned into \\(\\mathcal T_L\\) and \\(\\mathcal T_R\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 gives \\(|\\mathcal T_L|\\ge n-1\\), Lemma\u202f3 gives \\(|\\mathcal T_R|\\ge n-1\\), and the two families are disjoint.  Hence any admissible placement uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(R\\).*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(R\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(L\\).\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) cover every unit square of the \\(n\\times n\\) board except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares, each of which lies alone in its row and column.  No two rectangles overlap, and each rectangle\u2019s sides lie on grid lines, so the placement satisfies all the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nCorollary\u202f4 shows that any admissible placement needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles suffice.  For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
