s at least \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\n#### 4. Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nThe vector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nM\\mathbf{1}=(J-I)\\mathbf{1}=(n-1)\\mathbf{1},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) is an eigenvector with eigenvalue \\(n-1\\).\n\nFor any vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) we have \\(J\\mathbf{v}=0\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nM\\mathbf{v}= -\\mathbf{v},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nso \\(-1\\) is an eigenvalue of multiplicity \\(n-1\\).\n\nAll eigenvalues are non\u2011zero, therefore \\(M\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) and (9) we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}|\\ge n .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n#### 5. Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}\\bigr).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(T_i\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) that occupies the whole row \\(i\\) except the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Disjointness:* distinct rows give disjoint sets of squares.  \n*Avoidance of uncovered squares:* each \\(T_i\\) omits precisely the square \\((i,i)\\), which is uncovered.  \n*Coverage:* any square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r\\neq c\\) lies in \\(T_r\\); thus every covered square belongs to exactly one tile.\n\nTherefore the family \\(\\{T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\}\\) is a valid tiling consisting of \\(n\\) tiles, meeting the required condition that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\n#### 6. Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (10) shows that at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary, and the explicit tiling above shows that \\(n\\) tiles suffice. Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
