R\\,v_C^{\\mathsf T}\\) with the binary column vectors  \n\n\\[\nu_R(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{i\\in R\\}},\\qquad \nv_C(j)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{j\\in C\\}},\n\\]\n\n\\(M_{T}\\) has (real) rank \\(1\\).  \nSince the tiles are disjoint and cover exactly \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}M_{T_k}=J-P, \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all\u2011ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\(P_{i,\\sigma(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are \\(0\\)).  \n\nThe rank of the right\u2011hand side is computed as follows.  \n\\(J\\) has eigenvalue \\(n\\) with eigenvector \\(\\mathbf 1\\) and eigenvalue \\(0\\) on the orthogonal complement of \\(\\mathbf 1\\).  \n\\(P\\) is orthogonal; its eigenvalues are the \\(n\\)\u2011th roots of unity, none of which is \\(0\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathbf 1\\) is also an eigenvector of \\(P\\) with eigenvalue \\(1\\), and for any vector \\(x\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\) we have \\(Jx=0\\) and \\(Px\\neq0\\).  \nThus every eigenvalue of \\(J-P\\) is non\u2011zero, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and the sub\u2011additivity of rank,\n\n\\[\nn=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_k})\n      =\\sum_{k=1}^{m}1=m .\n\\]\n\nHence any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least \\(n\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\nm\\ge n=2025. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFix any permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\in[n]\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl([n]\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile; it covers every square of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nBecause the uncovered squares lie in distinct rows, the \\(n\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(C\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, showing that the lower bound (4) is sharp:\n\n\\[\nm_{\\min}=n=2025. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  All intermediate numbers of tiles are attainable\n\nThe construction in \u00a72 uses, for each row \\(i\\), a single interval of columns.  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq1,n\\) the interval splits naturally into a left part \\(\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\) and a right part \\(\\{i\\}\\times\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nEven when \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) or \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) the interval has length \\(n-1\\ge2\\) and can be cut at any interior grid line.  \n\nHence any existing rectangle can be divided into two smaller rectangles (still avoiding the uncovered square) by a vertical cut.  \nEach such division increases the total number of tiles by exactly one while preserving all required uncovered squares.\n\nStarting from the optimal tiling with \\(n\\) tiles, perform a sequence of such cuts until every covered square becomes a \\(1\\times1\\) tile.  \nThe final configuration uses \\(n(n-1)\\) tiles (the obvious upper bound, because there are \\(n(n-1)\\) covered unit squares).  \nSince each cut raises the tile count by one, every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n,\\;n+1,\\;n+2,\\dots ,\\;n(n-1)\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nappears as the number of tiles of some admissible tiling.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2025\\).  \nMoreover, for the \\(2025\\times2025\\) board every integer between \\(2025\\) and \\(2025\\cdot2024\\) inclusive can be realized as the number of tiles in a tiling satisfying the given row\u2011 and column\u2011uniqueness condition.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]  \n\nAll intermediate values \\(2025\\le k\\le2025\\cdot2024\\) are achievable.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nThe solution above has been reviewed for logical completeness and adherence to the problem requirements.  \nAll mathematical statements 